# What each absorbing buffer was asked to hold, beside how much it holds. One row per
# (interfering layer, buffer it named), carrying the shares attributed to that buffer and the
# slack filed for it.
#
# The unserved holders are left out
#   The other holders name somebody who absorbed the remainder, so their shares have to fit inside
#   the buffer that did the absorbing. `customer` and `unrealised` name demand nobody met: it is
#   the overflow, not a load on the buffer, and counting it would bound the wrong quantity.
#
# The served side comes from its own file
#   The served holders come from entries/served_holders.sqlc, every holder that is not one of the
#   unserved pair. A list of kinds written here, such as `kind <> 'unrealised'`, would leave out
#   one of the pair and add `customer`, demand that was made and went unserved, to the buffer's
#   load. checks/clearance_with_unserved.sqlc reads the holders this relation leaves out and calls
#   that same pair a violation, and entries/unserved_holders.sqlc is where the pair is named. How
#   the five kinds divide is decided once, in one place.
#
# Interference only
#   `pressed` also admits `transition`, and this relation keeps interference alone. Under a
#   transition the holders' shares carry the clearance side, which is the larger one and the one
#   the filer can count, while the buffer's slack bounds the interference side, so the comparison
#   would set two opposite edges of one buffer against each other. `Fit` says the two sides move
#   in opposite directions and must be read at one corner, and nothing in this relation can pick
#   it. checks/exposure_unaccounted.sqlc is the rule for that case: it reaches the same layers
#   through the exposure instead, and suspends rather than passing when the unserved share is
#   unstated.
#
# Holding is not absorbing
#   The schema says so above `HolderKind`. Which holders absorbed is not a fact the type carries.
#   It is read here from the sign and the unserved kinds, and that reading belongs to this file
#   rather than to any filing.
#
# Only a sized slack bounds anything
#   A buffer filed `unmeasured` has nothing to compare against, so its rows are left out rather
#   than passing: a bound with nothing to bound passes loudest.
#
# The sum, and what it leaves out
#   `borne_low`, `borne` and `borne_high` are folds/served_totals.sqlc's served shares added up at
#   the low, the most likely value and the high, one row per layer, so the served shares are added
#   up once. `sum()` skips NULLs, so a layer where a served share is filed `unmeasured`, or as
#   `sharesSum`'s output, sums to less than it holds. `unstated` is carried beside the sum, and a
#   reader bounding the load sets those layers aside rather than reading part of the load as all of
#   it.
#
# Both ranges, whole
#   The slack is carried at its three points beside the load. A rule bounding the load reads the
#   two ranges at opposite ends, the smallest load against the largest slack and the largest load
#   against the smallest, so both are carried whole.
#
# The buffer is reached through a reader's assertion
#   The absorber is a borrowed term. That it names one of the three buffers is a judgement recorded
#   in layers/absorber.sqlc, not a fact any filing states.
#
# Why `share_exceeds_slack` examines nothing
#   The served filter empties it, and that is a finding about the evidence, not a gap in it. Every
#   pressed layer left after `slack is sized` and `sign = interference` is then removed by the
#   served filter, because every holder on it is `customer` or `unrealised`. In this corpus those
#   layers are `enterprise-contract/capability`, `contrato-empresarial/capacidade-entregavel` and
#   `merge-holding-composition/shift-line`. So on every interference layer here that sizes its
#   absorbing buffer, nothing was absorbed at all: the demand was turned away. There are no served
#   shares to add up because no share was served, which is this model's own subject, stated by
#   the data.
#
# Check the zero rather than reading it
#   A `0` here comes from a working served filter, from a broken one, and from an earlier filter
#   that emptied the population: three different facts under one number. algebra/borne.sqlc
#   settles it without touching this file: on every run, for each layer, what the holders hold
#   equals what was absorbed plus what went unserved, at each of the three points. Run it for the
#   numbers; its `detail` reads `[<low>, <most likely>, <high>] held: [...] absorbed, [...]
#   unserved` per layer.
#
# Probe
#   Follow the population stage by stage (pressed, absorber and holders kept, `slack is sized`,
#   `sign = interference`, the served filter) and see where it empties. Disable the served filter
#   and rows appear, each against a slack of its own. That is not the rule waking up; it is the
#   rule counting turned-away demand as absorbed. A probe by hand cannot settle the zero the way
#   algebra/borne.sqlc does: a `sed` that matches nothing reports the opposite in silence.
-- pm:Remainder/pm:holder summed against the slack it names, keyed by pm:Remainder/pm:absorber.
SELECT b.filing, b.layer, b.buffer,
       b.borne_low, b.borne, b.borne_high,
       s.low  AS slack_low,
       s.mode AS slack_mode,
       s.high AS slack_high,
       s.unit AS slack_unit,
       b.unstated
FROM (
    SELECT p.filing, p.layer, a.buffer,
           h.served_low  AS borne_low,
           h.served_mode AS borne,
           h.served_high AS borne_high,
           h.unstated
    FROM      (
        :compose(layers/pressed.sqlc)
    ) p
    JOIN      (
        :compose(layers/absorber.sqlc)
    ) a USING (filing, layer)
    JOIN      (
        :compose(folds/served_totals.sqlc)
    ) h USING (filing, layer)
    WHERE p.sign = 'interference'
) b
JOIN (
    :compose(entries/slacks.sqlc)
) s USING (filing, layer, buffer)
WHERE s.mode IS NOT NULL
